\title{Disjointness bounds the rank at an interpolation node}
\taxon{Lemma}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{[Polynomial setup](cn-0019); [Evaluation identity](cn-0020); [Polynomial matrices and minors](cn-0077); [Quotient maps and restriction rank](cn-0082)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), equation (2.5); reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026. [Lean source: NodeRankLoss](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/NodeRankLoss.lean).}

\p{\strong{Statement.} In the [polynomial setup](cn-0019), fix #{j\in\{1,\ldots,m\}} and #{h\geq0}, and let #{V_j\subseteq W} be a subspace with #{U_j\cap V_j=0}. Then}

##{\dim E_h(W;t_j)\leq k_h(W)-k_h(V_j).}

\p{\strong{Proof.} Let #{\pi_j:W\to W/V_j} be the [quotient map](cn-0082). For a non-node #{t}, a constrained polynomial with coefficients in #{V_j} is also one with coefficients in #{W}, and its value lies in #{V_j}. Hence}

##{E_h(V_j;t)\subseteq E_h(W;t)\cap V_j
=\ker\bigl(\pi_j|_{E_h(W;t)}\bigr).}

\p{The [restriction formula](cn-0082) and [evaluation identity](cn-0020) give #{\dim\pi_j(E_h(W;t))=k_h(W)-\dim(E_h(W;t)\cap V_j)}. The displayed inclusion bounds the subtracted dimension below by #{k_h(V_j)}, so}

##{\dim\pi_j(E_h(W;t))\leq k_h(W)-k_h(V_j).}

\p{Choose a fixed basis of #{\mathcal P_h(W)} and coordinates in #{W/V_j}. The scalar coordinate polynomials of the projected basis maps form the columns of a [polynomial matrix](cn-0077). Its evaluation at #{t} has column space #{\pi_j(E_h(W;t))}. Every [minor](cn-0077) of order larger than the displayed rank bound vanishes at all non-nodes, an infinite set, and is therefore the zero polynomial. Evaluating these same minors at #{t_j} gives the same rank bound there. Finally #{E_h(W;t_j)\subseteq U_j} and [disjointness](cn-0076) #{U_j\cap V_j=0} imply #{E_h(W;t_j)\cap V_j=0}. The [restriction formula](cn-0082) therefore gives #{\dim\pi_j(E_h(W;t_j))=\dim E_h(W;t_j)}. Combining this equality with the specialized rank bound proves the claim.}

\p{The proof uses vanishing polynomial minors to specialize the rank bound. It does not assume that a generic basis stays independent at a [constraint node](cn-0063).}
